From b45d5117cf56bb39697e2249f90d04c5ecb2417b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fanboy <fanboy@openwrt.org>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 07:53:16 -1000
Subject: [PATCH] airoha: adapt smartrg 992-20/992-21 patches (@cmonroe) for
 w1700k

What 992-20-net-airoha-stability.patch does (drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/
airoha_eth.c + airoha_eth.h):

* Source-port routing in airoha_en7581_get_dev_from_sport() and
  airoha_an7583_get_dev_from_sport(): map pingpong (sport 0x0..0x1) and
  npu pingpong (sport 0x7) hardware source ports to GDM1 (port 0) the
  same way GDM1 (sport 0x10..0x14) is already handled. Previously these
  sports fell through to the default, returned -EINVAL, and
  airoha_qdma_get_gdm_dev() translated that into ERR_PTR(-ENODEV) ->
  packet drop. PON-internalised and NPU-tunneled RX traffic is now
  delivered to the LAN path instead of being silently dropped.

* RX IRQ bank-0 routing: RX_IRQ0_BANK_PIN_MASK changes 0x839f ->
  0xbb9f, adding BIT(11)|BIT(12)|BIT(13) to bank-0's RX DONE IRQ enable
  in airoha_qdma_hw_init(). All 32 RX queues get descriptors reserved
  because airoha_qdma_init_rx() iterates the full 32 entries against
  the all-ones RX_DONE_INT_MASK; the bit-selective IRQ bank mask,
  however, was upstream only enabling RX DONE interrupts for queues
  {0..4, 7..9, 15} (in bank-0), {21..30} (bank-1), and {5, 6} (banks
  2/3), leaving queues {10..14} descriptor-allocated but IRQ-masked
  - any packet landing there would never schedule NAPI. queue 11
  already gets 128 descriptors (RX_DSCP_NUM(11)==128 upstream) and
  queues 10,12,13,14 get the 16-descriptor default; this patch wires
  the DONE IRQs of 11,12,13 onto bank-0 so that traffic those queues
  do receive actually triggers RX NAPI. No effect if the w1700k never
  steers traffic onto those queues; harmless on the idle path.

* TX queue locking: spin_lock_bh/spin_unlock_bh -> spin_lock_irq/
  spin_unlock_irq on q->lock in airoha_qdma_tx_irq(),
  airoha_qdma_tx_flush(), and the q->lock acquisition paths inside
  airoha_tx_xmit() (xmit entry, tx-stopped early exit, xmit success
  exit, error-unlock fall-through). The lock is taken from NAPI poll
  (softirq) and from ndo_start_xmit (process context with bh disabled);
  it is never acquired from the airoha_irq_handler hard-IRQ handler,
  which only schedules NAPI. The _irq variant is therefore strictly
  more conservative than necessary; it's retained as a defensive
  hardening against softirq/runtime races observed in the original
  SmartRG stream the patches were sourced from.

* TRTCM parameter write: airoha_qdma_set_trtcm_param() now wraps the
  REG_TRTCM_DATA_LOW/REG_TRTCM_CFG_PARAM write pair in a do/while
  retry loop (up to 100 iterations) that does a write barrier and then
  re-reads via airoha_qdma_get_trtcm_param() to verify the value
  landed, breaking on match. This covers an observed silicon write-
  race on the egress TRTCM rate/bucket registers; the final
  read_poll_timeout wait for TRTCM_PARAM_RW_DONE is unchanged. Pure
  MMIO writeback safety net for token-bucket parameter programming
  via the QDMA TRTCM register block.

* ethtool/ethtool_stats groundwork: add union airoha_qdma_count {rx{
  rx_count, rx_error_count}, tx{tx_count, tx_error_count}} plus a count
  member on struct airoha_queue. RX loop bumps rx_count on success and
  rx_error_count on the free_frag path; TX IRQ loop bumps tx_count.
  No reader is currently present in this tree (the consumer lived in
  the trimmed-out extra_ops.ethtool_stats hook); the counters are
  write-only placeholders so future debug-stat consumers can be wired
  in without further header churn. Renamed patch keeps them since
  the struct addition is layout-neutral and the increments are cheap.

Explicitly NOT carried over from the smartrg B001 patch:
  - struct arht_extra_ops and all eth->extra_ops.* call sites
    (arht_rx_handler, arht_tx_handler, arht_hw_init, get_qdma_channel,
     get_qdma_buf_size, set_mac_addr, set_bucket_size, modify_ppe_entry,
     update_shrink_table, set_flow_dst, get_gdm_dev, tx_modified,
     sel_phy_external, support_eth_monitor, set_channel_retire,
     set_qdma_regs, set_qbi_fttr_chn, set_fe_regs, set_ppe_dft_cport,
     rx_dscp_num_init, ppe_exit, ethtool_stats)
  - pcs-handle_OF-phandle parse / dev->xfi_mac dev_get_regmap() lookup
    (the upstream airoha PCS driver registers regmaps named pcs_mac,
    hsgmii_an, hsgmii_pcs, multi_sgmii, usxgmii, pcs_pma, pcs_ana -
    never "xfi_mac" - so the lookup would always silently set
    dev->xfi_mac = NULL; dead code)
  - struct airoha_foe_bridge32, struct hop_info, struct action_info
    FOE entry layout refactor + field access changes
  - u32 meter -> u16 meter + u16 tunnel_mtu FOE entry field split
    (and the matching *tunnel_mtu |= FIELD_PREP(AIROHA_FOE_TUNNEL_MTU
    >> 16, ...) write in airoha_ppe.c)
  - The non-static promotion of airoha_ppe_foe_get_entry_hash,
    airoha_ppe_foe_get_entry_locked, airoha_ppe_foe_compare_entry,
    airoha_ppe_foe_commit_entry, airoha_ppe_offload_setup, and
    airoha_qdma_get_trtcm_param
  - AIROHA_MAX_NUM_CHANNELS=32, AIROHA_NUM_NETDEV_TX_RINGS widening,
    AIROHA_NUM_QOS_CHANNELS -> AIROHA_MAX_NUM_CHANNELS in the HTB
    tc_classid modulo paths and DECLARE_BITMAP widths
    (would let user space allocate up to 32 HTB QoS channels while
     the w1700k's EN7581 QDMA meter/group budget is not verified to
     support that many; left at upstream AIROHA_NUM_QOS_CHANNELS=4)
  - HW_DSCP_NUM 2048 -> 16384 (8x larger reserved-memory DMA pool for
    hardware-forwarding descriptors - not paid for in the w1700k DTS
    reserved-memory reservation)
  - LRO_RX_DSCP_NUM=128, AIROHA_HEADROOM, AIROHA_MAC_ADDR_NUM=7,
    FE_GDM_GDMA1..4 enum, FE_PSE_PORT_MCST=0xe, AIROHA_RXD4_SPORT
  - enum airoha_serdes_idx + dma_srcport/fport/nbq/chnl/retire_channel/
    gdm routing tables on airoha_eth_soc_data
  - ppe_sram_etry_num, qdma_init, work_started, meter_enable,
    reduce_memory fields on airoha_eth
  - dev->xfi_mac, port->eth fields and their assignments
  - enum magic {PON, ETH2, LAN1..4} and the e_magic/tx_modified/
    ingress_dev_idx/tid fields on airoha_flow_table_entry

What 992-21-net-airoha-npu-init-stability.patch does (drivers/net/
ethernet/airoha/airoha_npu.c):

* WLAN mailbox retry wrapper: factor airoha_npu_wlan_cmd_with_retry():
  3 retries with mdelay(10) between attempts; each attempt dispatches
  either airoha_npu_wlan_msg_send() (when is_msg_send) or
  airoha_npu_wlan_set_reserved_memory() (otherwise). Returns last
  error on exhaustion or 0 on success. Routes the five wlan mailbox
  setup commands in airoha_npu_wlan_init_memory()
  (WLAN_FUNC_SET_WAIT_NPU_BAND0_ONCPU,
   WLAN_FUNC_SET_WAIT_TX_BUF_CHECK_ADDR,
   WLAN_FUNC_SET_WAIT_PKT_BUF_ADDR,
   WLAN_FUNC_SET_WAIT_TX_PKT_BUF_ADDR, and
   WLAN_FUNC_SET_WAIT_IS_FORCE_TO_CPU) through the wrapper so any
  transient mailbox failure is retried rather than aborting wlan
  bring-up outright. The WLAN_FUNC_SET_WAIT_DRAM_BA_NODE_ADDR op
  (conditional on memory-region-names "ba") is left single-shot,
  matching upstream.

* Mailbox wait timeout: airoha_npu_send_msg() regmap_read_poll_timeout
  timeout is reduced 1000 * MSEC_PER_SEC -> 500 * MSEC_PER_SEC. The
  3-retry wrapper preserves the same aggregate worst-case wait
  budget while giving up on a hung mailbox sooner so the retry pass
  can re-issue.

Explicitly NOT carried over from the smartrg B002 patch:
  - airoha_npu.h header extraction (kept macros/enums/structs local
    in airoha_npu.c)
  - airoha_npu_wait_init_complete() init-completion gate (CR_NPU_MIB31 /
    INIT_COMPELET poll in airoha_npu_wlan_init_memory)
  - non-static promotion of airoha_npu_send_msg,
    airoha_npu_wlan_msg_send, airoha_npu_wlan_msg_get
  - PPE_FUNC_SET_WAIT_L4S_SETUP enum entry, struct ppe_mbox_data.l4s_info,
    struct tr471_mbox_data, WLAN_MAX_STATS_SIZE
  - extern declarations airoha_npu_l4s_setup and airoha_npu_extra_init
